==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|biker}}, from {{etyl|non|en}} {{m|non|bikarr||cup}}, from {{etyl|osx|en}} {{m|osx|bikeri||cup}}, from {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{m|la|bīcārium||wine vat, jug}}, of disputed origin. Possibly from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{m|grc|βίκος||earthenware jug, wine jar}}, or from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|bacarium||wine vat, vase}}. Cognate with {{cog|stq|Bieker||mug, cup, beaker}}, {{cog|nl|beker||beaker, cup}}, {{cog|de|Becher||beaker, cup, goblet}}, {{cog|da|bæger||beaker}}, Italian {{m|it|bicchiere||cup, glass (for drink)}}. See also {{l|en|pitcher}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈbiːkə(ɹ)/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈbiːkɚ/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|En-au-beaker.ogg|Audio (AU)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|iːkə(r)|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A flat-bottomed [[vessel]], with a [[lip]], used as a [[laboratory]] [[container]].
# A [[drinking]] vessel without a [[handle]], sometimes for the use of [[children]].
# A [[mug]].
# {{lb|en|slang|Antarctica}} A [[scientist]].
#* {{quote-text|lang=en|year=2008|author=Kim Stanley Robinson|title=Antarctica|page=52|passage={{...}} at every meal break he shambled into the galley black-fingered and smelling of engine-grease and concrete floors, to contemplate over his meal the '''beakers''' at their round tables chatting away, completely oblivious {{...}}}}

